SIGNAL = r"""{\sffamily\bfseries\small\color{Deep}In 55,223 hemodialysis patients across California, Oregon and Washington, a smoke plume over the clinic raised same-day mortality (RR 1.19, 1.11--1.27), but plume plus an extreme heat event gave RR 1.92 (1.71--2.17) against 1.02 (0.95--1.10) for plume alone; per 10\,$\mu$g/m$^3$ of wildfire PM$_{2.5}$ the mortality RR was 1.03 (1.02--1.06).}\\[1.4mm]
\footnotesize
University of Maryland, \textit{Environmental Epidemiology}; Fresenius Kidney Care records 2010--2018, time-stratified case-crossover, conditional quasi-Poisson, exposures assigned to the dialysis facility.
\textbf{Where it is weak:} exposure is clinic-sited, not residential, and wildfire PM$_{2.5}$ is a modelled split of total PM$_{2.5}$; the plume-alone null with a large joint effect means the headline plume signal is mostly heat-coincident, and the abstract gives no wildfire-PM$_{2.5}$ $\times$ heat interaction on the concentration scale.
\textbf{Why it matters here:} dialysis patients are at the same address three times a week. A low-cost monitor on each clinic is an exact exposure point for one of the most smoke-vulnerable populations, and it would replace the modelled split with a measured one."""

